{"name":"San Francisco Mauve","hex":"#936a6d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"san","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Sanskrit."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Ancient Greek letter representing /s/ (uppercase Ϻ, lowercase ϻ), originally used in some dialects instead of sigma."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/san"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"San Francisco","blocks":[{"text":"San Francisco, officially the City and County of San Francisco, is the 17th-most populous city and second-densest large city in the United States, with a population of 826,079 in 2025. It is the fourth-largest city in California. Some 4.6 million residents live in the city's metropolitan statistical area, which is the 13th-largest in the United States. Around 9.2 million live in the San Jose–San Francisco–Oakland combined statistical area, the fifth-largest in the United States. San Francisco is a global center for technology and finance, and is well-known for its affluence, eclectic architecture, and mild climate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Zeppelin-ride-020100925-195_%285029394846%29.jpg/330px-Zeppelin-ride-020100925-195_%285029394846%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Francisco"},{"title":"Francisco","blocks":[{"text":"Francisco is the Spanish and Portuguese form of the masculine given name Franciscus."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisco"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg/500px-San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg/1280px-San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg/500px-San_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASan_Francisco_centered_around_Market_St.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/San_Francisco%2C_CA_USA_-_Mission_San_Francisco_de_Asis_%281776%29_and_Mission_Dolores_Basilica_%281918%29_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg/500px-San_Francisco%2C_CA_USA_-_Mission_San_Francisco_de_Asis_%281776%29_and_Mission_Dolores_Basilica_%281918%29_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/San_Francisco%2C_CA_USA_-_Mission_San_Francisco_de_Asis_%281776%29_and_Mission_Dolores_Basilica_%281918%29_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg/1280px-San_Francisco%2C_CA_USA_-_Mission_San_Francisco_de_Asis_%281776%29_an